{"cut_proposal":{"candidate_errors":[],"prefix":"To find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) such that \\(\\mathbf{M}^3 = \\begin{pmatrix} 19 & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we start by finding the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}^3\\). The eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}^3\\) are the cubes of the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\).\n\nFirst, we find the characteristic polynomial of \\(\\mathbf{M}^3\\):\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{M}^3 - \\lambda \\mathbf{I}) = \\det\\begin{pmatrix} 19 - \\lambda & 30 \\\\ -45 & -71 - \\lambda \\end{pmatrix} = (19 - \\lambda)(-71 - \\lambda) - (-45)(30).\n\\]\nSimplifying the determinant, we get:\n\\[\n(19 - \\lambda)(-71 - \\lambda) + 1350 = \\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 1350.\n\\]\nSo, the characteristic polynomial is \\(\\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 1350\\). We solve the quadratic equation \\(\\lambda^2 + 52\\lambda + 1350 = 0\\) using the quadratic formula \\(\\lambda = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\\[\n\\lambda = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{52^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot 1350}}{2 \\cdot 1} = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{2704 - 5400}}{2} = \\frac{-52 \\pm \\sqrt{-2696}}{2} = \\frac{-52 \\pm 2i\\sqrt{674}}{2} = -26 \\pm i\\sqrt{674}.\n\\]\nThe eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}^3\\) are \\(-26 + i\\sqrt{674}\\) and \\(-26 - i\\sqrt{674}\\). Let the eigenvalues of \\(\\mathbf{M}\\) be \\(\\alpha\\) and \\(\\beta\\). Then \\(\\alpha^3 = -26 + i\\sqrt{674}\\) and \\(\\beta^3 = -26 - i\\sqrt{674}\\).\n\n","prefix_end":1262,"status":"review_required"},"eligible":true,"generation":{"generation":{"max_new_tokens":2048,"temperature":0.7,"top_k":0,"top_p":1.0},"ids":[1249,1477,279,6172,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,44,11035,8,1741,429,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,44,92,61,18,284,1124,7265,90,5187,2555,92,220,16,24,609,220,18,15,24984,481,19,20,609,481,22,16,1124,408,90,5187,2555,11035,701,582,1191,553,9271,279,28724,3661,315,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,44,92,61,18,59,568,576,28724,3661,315,1124,11520,10374,13233,90,44,92,61,18,57758,525,279,54104,315,279,28724,3661,315,1124,11